          "status": "GOLD",
          "disclaimer": "Notice: This snippet is extracted from the open access paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.1186/s40468-024-00332-x?email=<INSERT_YOUR_EMAIL> or https://doi.org/10.1186/s40468-024-00332-x, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."
        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Assessment significantly influences students' learning experience by developing their perceptions and understanding of what is important, guiding their engagement with learning tasks, and influencing how this understanding could be applicable to future learning situations (Carless, 2015;Estaji & Safari, 2023;Schellekens et al., 2021). The literature has revealed a strong interrelationship among teaching, assessment, and learning (Panadero et al., 2018;Phakiti & Leung, 2024). The assessment practices, including assessment task type, assessment criteria, grading, and feedback, carried out in a particular assessment system and context influences learners' cognitive and metacognitive regulation processes. These regulation processes, such as setting goals, monitoring progress, and adjusting actions aligned with the requirements of goals, are identified as fundamental aspects of learning (Carless, 2015;Panadero et al., 2018). Due to the importance of assessment in the learning process, the association between assessment and learning is extensively studied, resulting into a growing body of research on assessment practices and learning (Schellekens et al., 2021). \n\nThe construct of classroom-based assessment (CBA) is associated with Michael Scriven (1967), who first used the terms formative and summative evaluation. His work focused on the evaluation of curricula. Two years later, Benjamin Bloom (1969) suggested applying the concepts of \"formative and summative\" to evaluating student learning, which we call \"assessment\" today. The notions formative and summative assessments are assigned distinct roles: formative assessments allow for feedback aiming to aid and enhance student learning, whereas summative assessments are used to measure students' learning for accountability, ranking, or certification purposes (Black et al., 2003;Estaji & Kogani, 2024;Heritage, 2010). \n\nThe current interest in CBA and its practice is, however, largely credited to evolving educational systems worldwide based on major education policy shifts in various countries. For example, reforms in England's educational system in the 1980s were based on a report of the Task Group on Assessment and Testing chaired by Paul Black, which found that the central government controlled the country's curriculum and assessment.",
