      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study uses the Systematic Literature Review (SLR) approach to evaluate the effectiveness of formative assessment in improving student learning outcomes. This approach was chosen because it allows a comprehensive analysis of relevant literature, as well as providing transparent guidance in the process of identifying, selecting, and analyzing data (Kitchenham & Charters, 2007). To ensure that the article selection process is carried out systematically and structured, the PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Luthfi Ilham Ramdhani/ Enhancing Student Learning Outcomes through Formative Assessment: A Systematic Literature Review Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) framework is used (Moher et al., 2009). This approach provides a clear structure in critically screening the literature. The research flow using the Prisma method is presented in Figure 1. This study used the Systematic Literature Review (SLR) approach with the PRISMA framework to ensure that article selection was carried out systematically and structured. At the identification stage, 300 articles were successfully obtained through searches using keywords such as \"formative assessment,\" \"learning outcomes,\" and \"feedback in education\" from various platforms such as Scopus, PubMed, Google Scholar, and ERIC. However, 200 articles were removed because they were duplicates, which often occurs because the same article appears in various databases due to overlapping metadata or republishing in different journals or conferences. In addition, 30 other articles were removed because they were not relevant to the research topic. In the next stage, from the remaining 70 articles, 40 articles were selected for further evaluation, but 5 articles were not accessible in full text form, and 5 other articles were only abstracts without sufficient information to be analyzed. These stages ensure that the screening process produces the most relevant articles for further review. \n\nTo ensure quality and relevance, the final 30 articles were selected based on strict inclusion and exclusion criteria. Inclusion criteria included articles that discussed the direct relationship between formative assessment and learning outcomes, used a strong methodology, and were published in a peerreviewed journal between 2019 and 2024. Conversely, exclusion criteria were applied to eliminate irrelevant articles, such as those that focused only on summative assessment, did not provide empirical data, or had weak methodologies.",
