      "snippet": {
        "text": "With regard to the quality of instruction in science and other domains, one focus of educational research is the teacher and his practices in class (Geller, Neumann, & Fischer, 2014;Seidel & Shavelson, 2007;Vieluf, Kaplan, Klieme, & Bayer, 2012). Science teachers need professional knowledge in order to plan, conduct and analyse their teaching and adapt it to their students' needs (Baumert & Kunter, 2013;Gess-Newsome, 2015). The adaptation of teaching to students' individual learning requires teachers to realise formative assessment (Bell & Cowie, 2001;Black & Wiliam, 1998;Cizek, 2010). Teachers' practices that are related to formative assessment like interpreting students' ideas or providing feedback are relevant for successful student learning (Furtak et al., 2016;Gotwals & Birmingham, 2016;Millar, 2017). The proficient enactment of formative assessment practices by a teacher can lead to comparatively large gains in student achievement (Black & Wiliam, 1998;Hattie & Timperley, 2007;Kingston & Nash, 2011).\n\nThe present study focuses on two important aspects of formative assessment practices, on pre-service teachers' judgement accuracy and on the quantity and quality of their written feedback. Providing feedback is some kind of a bridge between teachers' intended learning goals and students' current levels of achievement in relation to those learning goals (Hattie & Timperley, 2007). Analysing pre-service teachers' judgements and the feedback they give based on this judgements, can provide insights into pre-service teachers' formative assessment practices.",
