      "snippet": {
        "text": "Definitions of formative assessment emphasise the importance of continuous assessments for student learning: 'Formative assessment, also referred to as assessment for learning, is a process in which teachers and students recognise and respond to student learning, during that learning' (Cowie, 2012, p. 679). Gotwals and Birmingham (2016) identified four practices that together describe a well enactment of formative assessment on the teachers' side: Teachers (1) 'provide opportunities for students to demonstrate their understandings', (2) 'identify students' ideas', (3) 'interpret students' responses', and (4) 'respond to students' ideas with feedback and/or instructional decisions that will help move from or build on current understandings' (p. 366; see also Furtak et al., 2016;Haug & Ødegaard, 2015). Research in the German speaking countries has tended to focus more on a specific aspect of these practices called teachers' judgement accuracy (Anders, Kunter, Brunner, Krauss, & Baumert, 2010;Schrader, 2013). Judgement in this case means that the teacher has to interpret accurately what a piece of student work (i.e. what was said, written or created) implies with respect to the achievement of specific learning goals or the progress towards these goals (Dolin, Black, Harlen, & Tiberghien, 2018). Accurately involves that the teacher uses criteria to judge the student work and that it is possible to quantify somehow the judgement, i.e. based on rubrics (Panadero & Jonson, 2013). Thus, judgement accuracy is an integral part of teachers' formative assessment practices since the identification and interpretation of student ideas and responses also includes a judgement. The practices, however, stress the relevance to enact a particular response much more than judgment accuracy does as they reflect the process-like character of formative assessment and as they integrate feedback into this process (Cowie, 2012;Furtak et al., 2016).\n\nConsequently, formative assessment practices are an essential part of teachers' professional competence. Models of teachers' professional competence reflect this importance by defining facets that encompass formative assessment practices (Baumert & Kunter, 2013;Gess-Newsome, 2015",
