        "text": "Both these and other studies (Bok et al., 2013;De Silva, 2023;DeLuca & Volante, 2016;Holmes et al., 2021) emphasize that competency-based assessment that draws on the learning assessment framework requires meaningful feedback with each assessment. Particular attention should be paid to the quality of feedback at each assessment point. This study provides empirical evidence of the impact of a competency-based assessment model for pre-service teachers to be deployed in remote and challenging school settings. This model is designed to be integrated into regular in-service teachers training practice. For the practical application of the research results, the following points should be noted: \n\n1. The results of competency-based assessments of pre-service teachers should be used to prepare students for their professional practice experiences in schools. If students' competencies do not meet curricular expectations, the institution should improve these competencies to ensure readiness for professional practice. 2. The process of competency-based assessment with teacher students should be appropriate for class sizes of no more than 25 students to ensure comprehensive feedback and the collection of diverse evidence of learning for individual competency improvement (Brown & Frontiers Media SA, 2019). 3. Teachers should have a positive attitude towards teaching, as the success of a competency-based curriculum depends on how teachers assess and use assessment results to improve student learning. Meaningful feedback should promote students' individual competencies. 4. Teachers should create a supportive and encouraging environment where students can openly discuss and accept their successes and failures on tasks without fear of blame or alienation from others.",
