      "snippet": {
        "text": "Answering the first research question required two steps. \n\nThe first step was to identify the teacher's formative assessment practice by analysing notes from classroom observations, the teacher's logs and the teacher interviews. The framework with key strategies by Wiliam and Thompson (2008) was used as a tool for analysis. Practices such as the following were noted: setting learning goals (KS 1), gathering information about learning progress (KS 2) providing adjusted instruction, feedback, or any other support, based on assessed learning needs, to bring the students to reach the learning goals (KS 3), peer-assessment or peer feedback (KS 4), and exercising selfregulation (KS 5). Activities that did not include any characteristics of formative assessment were not included in the next step of the analysis. \n\nThe internalization and integration of values and behaviours towards a more self-determined and integrated motivation is described as a process (Ryan & Deci, 2020), that is, a transformation over time, rather than a sudden change. Consequently, the second step was to determine whether or not each of the identified activities in step one was regularly used during the intervention. This was done by identifying, based on teacher's logs and teacher interviews, which of the previously identified formative activities were carried out regularly and during the whole, or a major part, of the intervention. An activity was determined as regularly used if it was described as such in both logs and interviews. Depending on the aim of the activity, it might be implemented every day, every week or be linked to recurrent events such as tests. \n\nThe second author conducted the two-step analysis to identify the teacher's regularly used formative assessment activities. In all uncertain cases, the fourth author conducted additional analyses. The analyzes of the uncertain cases were compared and discussed until consensus was reached.",
