      "snippet": {
        "text": "Yet there is also a growing body of evidence that early momentum metrics (such as completion of college-level courses in the first year and the number of credits completed in the first semester) are good predictors of longer-term outcomes like persistence and college completion (e.g. Belfield et al., 2019;Jenkins & Bailey, 2017;Zhang, 2022), which suggests that it may be possible for early gains from postsecondary reforms to promote momentum toward subsequent college success. \n\nFlorida provides an ideal context for exploring the extent to which statewide developmental education reform may contribute to improvements in longer-term outcomes like transfer and credential attainment. In 2014, Florida implemented one of the nation's most comprehensive developmental education reforms under Senate Bill (SB) 1720. Incoming college students who entered a Florida public high school in 2003-04 or later and completed a standard high school diploma-as well as active-duty military personnel-became exempt from college placement testing and could enroll directly into college-level courses regardless of their incoming level of preparation. Since most community college students attend local institutions, the majority of FTIC students at FCS institutions (approximately 65%) are classified as exempt. Nonexempt students who did not meet these criteria were still required to take developmental education courses if they scored below college-ready, but the colleges were required to offer these courses using accelerated instructional strategies such as a compressed model where students complete a developmental course in the first half of the semester and a college-level course in the same subject area in the second half of the semester. Colleges also had to develop enhanced advising and academic support services to help promote student success under the reform. \n\nEarly evidence suggests that Florida's reform has been effective at improving shortterm outcomes like initial